["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>For a few weeks a year, careful bulls and bucks get careless. Know the calendar and be there when it happens.</p></aside><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-elk-bull-side.jpg\" alt=\"Bull elk near Maligne Canyon, Jasper, Alberta. Dark mane, pale body, long swept back antlers with many tines.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bull elk near Maligne Canyon, Jasper, Alberta. Dark mane, pale body, long swept back antlers with many tines. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Wilson Hui from Calgary, Canada, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bull_Elk_Stag_(32272946993).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Elk first, then moose, then deer <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Your October hunts land in the deer <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Pre rut\">Pre rut</button> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what this session covers</summary><ul><li>The <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Rut\">Rut</button> is the breeding season. Males roam, fight, call and lose their caution <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Each species has its own calendar. Elk first, then moose, then deer. From late September to December something is rutting somewhere in BC <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>This session: timing for elk, moose, mule deer, white tails and black tails; <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Rattling\">Rattling</button>, calling and scents; the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> (Chronic Wasting Disease) rule on lures; and what changes for you week by week.</li><li>Your first hunts in mid to late October land in the deer <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Pre rut\">Pre rut</button> and the tail end of the moose rut <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 340\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Rut timeline by species, September to December. Elk: early September to mid October, peak about the third week of September. Moose: late September to mid October, peak last week of September to first week of October. White tailed deer: late October to early December, peak mid November. Mule deer: mid November to mid December, peak mid November. Black tailed deer: November to early December, peak mid November. Your mid to late October hunts land in the deer pre rut and the end of the moose rut.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"340\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Who ruts when</text>\n<rect x=\"212\" y=\"44\" width=\"37\" height=\"232\" fill=\"#f3d36b\" opacity=\".45\"/>\n<line x1=\"110\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"276\" stroke=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"142\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sep</text>\n<line x1=\"179\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"179\" y2=\"276\" stroke=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"212\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oct</text>\n<line x1=\"251\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"251\" y2=\"276\" stroke=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"284\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Nov</text>\n<line x1=\"321\" y1=\"44\" x2=\"321\" y2=\"276\" stroke=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"353\" y=\"296\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Dec</text>\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Elk</text>\n<rect x=\"115\" y=\"59\" width=\"97\" height=\"22\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" opacity=\".75\"/>\n<path d=\"M156 55 l8 15 l-8 15 l-8 -15 Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"121\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Moose</text>\n<rect x=\"163\" y=\"104\" width=\"49\" height=\"22\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" opacity=\".75\"/>\n<path d=\"M179 100 l8 15 l-8 15 l-8 -15 Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">White tail</text>\n<rect x=\"235\" y=\"149\" width=\"95\" height=\"22\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".75\"/>\n<path d=\"M284 145 l8 15 l-8 15 l-8 -15 Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"211\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Mule deer</text>\n<rect x=\"284\" y=\"194\" width=\"69\" height=\"22\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#c0581a\" opacity=\".75\"/>\n<path d=\"M290 190 l8 15 l-8 15 l-8 -15 Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"256\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\">Black tail</text>\n<rect x=\"251\" y=\"239\" width=\"79\" height=\"22\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\" opacity=\".75\"/>\n<path d=\"M284 235 l8 15 l-8 15 l-8 -15 Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<path d=\"M30 316 l7 -8 l7 8 l-7 8 Z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"321\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">peak</text>\n<rect x=\"110\" y=\"309\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#f3d36b\"/><text x=\"134\" y=\"321\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">your mid to late October hunts</text>\n</svg></figure><ul><li>Day length sets the date. Weather sets how much you see <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Cold, calm mornings: rutting animals on their feet <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the timing table and Region 3 notes</summary><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Rut window</th><th>Peak</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Elk</td><td>early September to mid October <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></td><td>about the third week of September, a smaller second wave in early October <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Moose</td><td>late September to mid October, young bulls later <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></td><td>last week of September to first week of October <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></td></tr><tr><td>White tailed deer</td><td>late October to early December <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td><td>mid November; conception about 13 to 17 November in eastern studies <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Mule deer</td><td>mid November to mid December <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td><td>mid November <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Black tailed deer (Region 2)</td><td>November to early December <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td><td>mid November <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Timing is driven by day length, so it barely moves year to year. Weather changes how much you <strong>see</strong>, not when it happens <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Cold, crisp, calm mornings put rutting animals on their feet in daylight. Warm wind hides them <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Region 3: no elk general open season is printed in the Region 3 pages, and moose general seasons are spike fork bulls only in some <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"MU\">MU</button> (Management Unit) areas <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. For you, the rut matters most for deer (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 372\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The deer rut in 4 phases. Pre rut, mid October to early November: bucks rub and check does at dawn and dusk. Seeking and chasing, first half of November: bucks cruise funnels in daylight. Peak, mid November: a buck tends one doe, hunt the does. Post rut, December: bucks thin and back in cover, a small second rut about 28 days later.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"372\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer rut, 4 phases</text>\n  <g data-tip=\"Buck activity|Bucks are most active from early to mid November. Mule deer bucks rut on winter range, so the rut and the snow bring them to the same open slopes.\">\n    <path d=\"M20 168 Q80 164 110 132 Q150 58 178 56 Q206 58 232 132 Q252 166 282 168 L380 170\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <text x=\"260\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b3470a\">buck activity</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-tip=\"Second rut|Does not bred cycle again about 28 days later, so a smaller second rut comes in December.\">\n    <path d=\"M276 168 Q296 140 316 168\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n    <text x=\"296\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2nd rut</text>\n  </g>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n    <path d=\"M100 202 v8 M165 202 v8 M240 202 v8 M305 202 v8\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/>\n    <text x=\"22\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"start\">15 Oct</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"226\">1 Nov</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"226\">15 Nov</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"226\">1 Dec</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"226\">15 Dec</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-tip=\"1. Pre rut|Mid October to early November. Bucks leave bachelor groups, rub, scrape (white tails) and check doe groups at dusk and dawn.\">\n    <rect x=\"20\" y=\"178\" width=\"88\" height=\"24\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/><text x=\"64\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"238\" width=\"188\" height=\"58\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"260\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"265\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Pre rut</text>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#f3d36b\">rubs, dusk checks</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-tip=\"2. Seeking and chasing|First half of November. Bucks travel, cruise funnels between doe groups and show up in daylight. Sit funnels and saddles all morning (Tip).\">\n    <rect x=\"108\" y=\"178\" width=\"52\" height=\"24\" fill=\"#e9a36f\"/><text x=\"134\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n    <rect x=\"204\" y=\"238\" width=\"188\" height=\"58\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"226\" cy=\"260\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#e9a36f\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n    <text x=\"246\" y=\"265\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Seeking</text>\n    <text x=\"246\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#f3d36b\">cruising funnels</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-tip=\"3. Peak|Mid November. A buck tends one doe at a time, follows her for a day or two and fights rivals. Does lead, bucks follow: hunt the does (Tip).\">\n    <rect x=\"160\" y=\"178\" width=\"70\" height=\"24\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"195\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"304\" width=\"188\" height=\"58\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"326\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"332\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"331\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Peak</text>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"352\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#f3d36b\">hunt the does</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-tip=\"4. Post rut|December. Bucks are thin, tired and back in cover. Snow pushes deer to winter range.\">\n    <rect x=\"240\" y=\"178\" width=\"140\" height=\"24\" fill=\"#6b7a55\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text>\n    <rect x=\"204\" y=\"304\" width=\"188\" height=\"58\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"226\" cy=\"326\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#6b7a55\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"332\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text>\n    <text x=\"246\" y=\"331\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Post rut</text>\n    <text x=\"246\" y=\"352\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#f3d36b\">thin, in cover</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Pre rut: rubs and bucks checking does at dusk <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Peak: does lead, bucks follow. Hunt the does (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: each phase in detail and my lean</summary><ul><li><strong>Pre rut, mid October to early November:</strong> bucks leave bachelor groups, rub, scrape (white tails), and start checking doe groups at dusk and dawn <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><strong>Seeking and chasing, first half of November:</strong> bucks travel, cruise funnels between doe groups, and show up in daylight <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li><strong>Peak, mid November:</strong> a buck tends one doe at a time, follows her for a day or two, and fights rivals <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>. Does lead, bucks follow: hunt the does (Tip).</li><li><strong>Post rut, December:</strong> bucks are thin, tired and back in cover. A second, smaller rut comes when unbred does cycle again about 28 days later <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Mule deer bucks rut on winter range, so the rut and the snow bring them down to the same open slopes <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>White tails rut near the fields and bottoms where the does live <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>Your October hunt is pre rut. Glass doe groups at dusk. The first bucks will be nosing around them. In November, sit a saddle or bench between doe groups from first light until 11.</p></aside></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-buck-4pt.jpg\" alt=\"Mule deer buck in the rut.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Mule deer buck in the rut. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Oregon Department of Fish &amp; Wildlife, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_buck_elk_creek_m_myatt_(5489214303).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wt-buck-side.jpg\" alt=\"White tailed buck.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>White tailed buck. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Famartin, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2025-11-09_07_48_17_White-tailed_Deer_buck_in_autumn_along_Aquetong_Lane_in_the_Mountainview_section_of_Ewing_Township,_Mercer_County,_New_Jersey.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Which calls are legal. Mouth and hand calls, rattling antlers and grunt tubes: legal for deer, elk and moose. Electronic or recorded calls: unlawful for deer, elk and moose, allowed only for wolf, coyote, cougar, lynx, bobcat and Schedule C birds. Synopsis page 12, item 41.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Which calls are legal?</text>\n  <rect x=\"10\" y=\"44\" width=\"185\" height=\"200\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\"/>\n  <rect x=\"205\" y=\"44\" width=\"185\" height=\"200\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f6dccb\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Mouth or hand</text>\n  <text x=\"297\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Electronic</text>\n  <rect x=\"60\" y=\"88\" width=\"70\" height=\"18\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><rect x=\"40\" y=\"92\" width=\"22\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n  <path d=\"M60 120 q20 -14 40 0 M80 125 q20 -14 40 0\" stroke=\"#8b5a2b\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/>\n  <rect x=\"265\" y=\"86\" width=\"64\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"106\" r=\"11\" fill=\"#6f6252\"/><rect x=\"302\" y=\"96\" width=\"20\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer, elk, moose</text>\n  <text x=\"102\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">✓ legal</text>\n  <text x=\"297\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer, elk, moose</text>\n  <text x=\"297\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"30\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#c0392b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">✗ illegal</text>\n  <text x=\"297\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">OK only: predators *</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">* wolf, coyote, cougar, lynx, bobcat, Schedule C birds</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Synopsis page 12, item 41 (95%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Call little, listen a lot (Tip).</li><li>Rattling: best in peak rut, cool calm mornings <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: rattling, grunts, elk and moose calls</summary><ul><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Rattling\">Rattling</button> copies two bucks fighting. In a Texas radio collar study about 65% of rattling sessions pulled in a buck, best in the peak rut, in the morning, cool and calm <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>Rattling works best where bucks compete for does, so it is less reliable for mule deer and on heavily hunted ground <span class=\"cert c-tip\" title=\"Certainty\">50%</span>.</li><li>Grunt tube: short soft grunts every 15 to 20 minutes in November. A doe bleat can stop a cruising buck (hunter lore) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Elk: a cow call is the safest call for a beginner. Bugling can pull a herd bull in or push him away (hunter lore) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Moose: a long cow moan at dusk, and raking a shoulder blade or paddle on brush to sound like a bull (hunter lore) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Recorded or electronic calls are unlawful for hunting wildlife except wolf, coyote, cougar, lynx, bobcat and Schedule C birds <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Mouth and hand calls only for deer, elk and moose.</li><li>Scents: skip deer urine lures. The rule is on the next screen <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>.</li></ul></details><a class=\"yt-card\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XK3cDSiUQMo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><span class=\"yt-play\">▶</span><span class=\"yt-text\"><b>Where, When and How to Rattle in More Bucks Using Science</b><small>Rattle during the pre rut, near cover, with the wind right.<br>National Deer Association, YouTube, 4 min. Needs internet.</small></span></a><a class=\"yt-card\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JcxAklUHW8I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><span class=\"yt-play\">▶</span><span class=\"yt-text\"><b>How To Use a Grunt Call To Call Big Bucks</b><small>Short soft grunts. Less is more.<br>Knight &amp; Hale, YouTube, 1 min. Needs internet.</small></span></a>","<aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 382\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Flowchart: can I use this lure? First question: is it made from deer, elk, moose or caribou, like urine or glands? If no, a synthetic lure is not a cervid part, my reading, 80 percent. If yes: did it come from outside BC? Bottled urine almost always does. If yes or unsure: unlawful to use for hunting, synopsis page 12 item 51, 95 percent. My lean: no urine lures. Play the wind.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"382\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"27\" font-size=\"18\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Can I use this lure?</text>\n<rect x=\"50.0\" y=\"40\" width=\"300\" height=\"58\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"64.5\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Made from deer, elk, moose</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"83.5\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">or caribou? (urine, glands)</text>\n\n<path d=\"M140 98 V128\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><path d=\"M140.0 134.0 L144.9 125.0 L135.1 125.0z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<path d=\"M290 98 V128\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><path d=\"M290.0 134.0 L294.9 125.0 L285.1 125.0z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<rect x=\"90.4\" y=\"104.0\" width=\"33.6\" height=\"27.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#2f6e3b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"107.209\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f6e3b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">No</text>\n<rect x=\"306.0\" y=\"104.0\" width=\"42.2\" height=\"27.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#c92a2a\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"327.124\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#c92a2a\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Yes</text>\n<rect x=\"12.0\" y=\"138\" width=\"176\" height=\"78\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e3eedc\" stroke=\"#2f6e3b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Synthetic</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">not a cervid part</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(my reading, 80%)</text>\n<rect x=\"198.0\" y=\"138\" width=\"184\" height=\"78\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"163\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">From outside BC?</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bottled urine</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"201\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">almost always is</text>\n<path d=\"M290 216 V248\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2.4\"/><path d=\"M290.0 254.0 L294.9 245.0 L285.1 245.0z\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<rect x=\"160.6\" y=\"222.0\" width=\"117.4\" height=\"27.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"219.319\" y=\"241\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Yes or unsure</text>\n<rect x=\"198.0\" y=\"258\" width=\"184\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#c92a2a\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"284.5\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Unlawful to use</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"303.5\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">page 12, item 51 (95%)</text>\n<rect x=\"8\" y=\"334\" width=\"384\" height=\"40\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"360\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">My lean: no urine lures. Play the wind.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li><button class=\"term\" data-term=\"CWD\">CWD</button> (Chronic Wasting Disease) can ride in urine scents <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the CWD rules in full and my lean</summary><ul><li>It is unlawful to use, for the purpose of hunting wildlife, any part or derivative of a deer, elk, moose or caribou if the part or derivative originated from outside British Columbia <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Synopsis page 12, item 51.</li><li>It is unlawful to possess any part of a Cervidae (caribou, deer, elk, moose) killed outside BC, other than the edible meat of the four quarters, loins, neck and ribs, or the hide, antlers and skull plate cleaned of all tissue <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Synopsis page 12, item 50.</li><li>The synopsis names urine based scents as a material that can carry CWD prions into new areas <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Synopsis page 81, Chronic Wasting Disease.</li><li>Bottled deer urine on store shelves is almost always from farms outside BC, so using it for hunting is an offence under item 51 (85%, interpretation). A synthetic lure is not a cervid derivative (80%, interpretation).</li><li>CWD was confirmed in BC in January 2024 in the Kootenay. Deer, elk and moose harvested in the CWD Management Zone (MUs 4-1 to 4-8 and 4-20 to 4-25) have mandatory testing and transport limits <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Synopsis page 15 (transport restrictions), page 81, and the Region 4 pages.</li><li>Region 3 is outside the zone, but the Region 3 pages ask hunters to submit deer, elk and moose heads for CWD testing and list a Kamloops drop off <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Region 3 synopsis page 32. See <a href=\"#/s/bc-rules\">BC rules</a>.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout lean\"><div class=\"callout-t\">My lean (opinion)</div><p>No urine lures at all. The upside is small and the rule is clear. Your scent control is the wind, not a bottle.</p></aside></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-bull-side.jpg\" alt=\"Bull moose: sit a willow swamp at dawn and dusk.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bull moose: sit a willow swamp at dawn and dusk. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jacob W. Frank, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alces_alces_1.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-elk-wallow.jpg\" alt=\"Elk wallow: rut sign at a wet meadow.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Elk wallow: rut sign at a wet meadow. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Loren Lane ( MORA ), Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elk_wallow._101981._slide_(f74312c976714e5db311641d02f847ac).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Spike fork bull moose: general seasons in some MUs (Management Units) <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Other moose and elk: <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"LEH\">LEH</button> (Limited Entry Hunting) or closed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<details class=\"more\"><summary>More: moose and elk seasons and tactics</summary><ul><li>Region 3 has general open seasons for <strong>spike fork</strong> bull moose in some MU areas, with dates between 20 September and 15 November depending on the MU <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Region 3 synopsis page 34. By MU (99%, Hunting Regulation, Schedule 3, items 11 to 11.2, BC Laws, checked 2026-10-17): 20 September to 31 October in 3-34 to 3-38, 3-40 to 3-44 and 3-46; 15 October to 15 November in 3-15 to 3-17 and 3-31 to 3-33; 1 to 15 November in 3-12 to 3-14, 3-18 to 3-20, 3-26 to 3-30 and 3-39.</li><li>A spike fork bull is a young bull whose antlers meet the synopsis definition. Read the definition on the synopsis definitions pages before you shoot <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Other moose hunts in Region 3 are by <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"LEH\">LEH</button> (Limited Entry Hunting) draw <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>No elk general open season appears in the Region 3 pages <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. The <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/limited-entry-hunting/leh-regulations-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LEH synopsis 2026 to 2027</a> lists no Region 3 elk hunt either, so there is no elk hunting in Region 3 this licence year (95%, read 2026-10-17).</li><li>Moose rut tactic: sit a willow swamp or lake edge at dawn and dusk from the last week of September, call softly, and listen for grunts and antler raking (hunter lore) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li><li>Elk rut tactic: find bugling at first light, get the wind, and cow call from 100 m back in cover (hunter lore) <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>.</li></ul></details><a class=\"yt-card\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jdTg-LOokes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><span class=\"yt-play\">▶</span><span class=\"yt-text\"><b>Moose Calling Techniques - How To (Eastmans' Hunting)</b><small>Hear real moose calls and when to use each one.<br>Eastmans Hunting Journals, YouTube, 5 min. Needs internet.</small></span></a>","<div class=\"regs\" data-regs=\"deerR3\"></div>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 372\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The deer rut in 4 phases. Pre rut, mid October to early November: bucks rub and check does at dawn and dusk. Seeking and chasing, first half of November: bucks cruise funnels in daylight. Peak, mid November: a buck tends one doe, hunt the does. Post rut, December: bucks thin and back in cover, a small second rut about 28 days later.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"372\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Deer rut, 4 phases</text>\n  <g data-tip=\"Buck activity|Bucks are most active from early to mid November. Mule deer bucks rut on winter range, so the rut and the snow bring them to the same open slopes.\">\n    <path d=\"M20 168 Q80 164 110 132 Q150 58 178 56 Q206 58 232 132 Q252 166 282 168 L380 170\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <text x=\"260\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#b3470a\">buck activity</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-tip=\"Second rut|Does not bred cycle again about 28 days later, so a smaller second rut comes in December.\">\n    <path d=\"M276 168 Q296 140 316 168\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\"/>\n    <text x=\"296\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2nd rut</text>\n  </g>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n    <path d=\"M100 202 v8 M165 202 v8 M240 202 v8 M305 202 v8\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"/>\n    <text x=\"22\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"start\">15 Oct</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"226\">1 Nov</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"226\">15 Nov</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"226\">1 Dec</text><text x=\"305\" y=\"226\">15 Dec</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-tip=\"1. Pre rut|Mid October to early November. Bucks leave bachelor groups, rub, scrape (white tails) and check doe groups at dusk and dawn.\">\n    <rect x=\"20\" y=\"178\" width=\"88\" height=\"24\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/><text x=\"64\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"238\" width=\"188\" height=\"58\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"260\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"265\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Pre rut</text>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#f3d36b\">rubs, dusk checks</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-tip=\"2. Seeking and chasing|First half of November. Bucks travel, cruise funnels between doe groups and show up in daylight. Sit funnels and saddles all morning (Tip).\">\n    <rect x=\"108\" y=\"178\" width=\"52\" height=\"24\" fill=\"#e9a36f\"/><text x=\"134\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n    <rect x=\"204\" y=\"238\" width=\"188\" height=\"58\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"226\" cy=\"260\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#e9a36f\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text>\n    <text x=\"246\" y=\"265\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Seeking</text>\n    <text x=\"246\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#f3d36b\">cruising funnels</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-tip=\"3. Peak|Mid November. A buck tends one doe at a time, follows her for a day or two and fights rivals. Does lead, bucks follow: hunt the does (Tip).\">\n    <rect x=\"160\" y=\"178\" width=\"70\" height=\"24\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"195\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n    <rect x=\"8\" y=\"304\" width=\"188\" height=\"58\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"326\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"332\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"331\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Peak</text>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"352\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#f3d36b\">hunt the does</text>\n  </g>\n  <g data-tip=\"4. Post rut|December. Bucks are thin, tired and back in cover. Snow pushes deer to winter range.\">\n    <rect x=\"240\" y=\"178\" width=\"140\" height=\"24\" fill=\"#6b7a55\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text>\n    <rect x=\"204\" y=\"304\" width=\"188\" height=\"58\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"226\" cy=\"326\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#6b7a55\"/><text x=\"226\" y=\"332\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text>\n    <text x=\"246\" y=\"331\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\">Post rut</text>\n    <text x=\"246\" y=\"352\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#f3d36b\">thin, in cover</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n</figure>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Weeks</th><th>Do this (Tip)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Late Sep to early Oct</td><td>Scout deer feed and water</td></tr><tr><td>Mid Oct</td><td>Glass doe groups at dusk</td></tr><tr><td>Late Oct</td><td>Sit a feed edge, dawn and dusk</td></tr><tr><td>Early Nov</td><td>Sit funnels and saddles all morning</td></tr><tr><td>Mid Nov</td><td>Find the does. Be patient</td></tr><tr><td>Late Nov to 10 Dec</td><td>Hunt winter range, track in snow</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the full week by week table</summary><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Weeks</th><th>What is happening</th><th>What you do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Late September to early October</td><td>Elk peak, moose peak. Deer still in summer pattern, bucks in bachelor groups <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></td><td>Moose or elk calling only if you hold a tag. Scout deer feed and water (Tip)</td></tr><tr><td>Mid October</td><td>Moose rut fading. Deer pre rut starts: rubs appear, bachelor groups split <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></td><td>Glass doe groups at dusk, note the first bucks, find rub lines (Tip)</td></tr><tr><td>Late October</td><td>White tail scrapes open. Mule deer bucks begin checking does. Snow up high starts moving deer down <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></td><td>Your first deer hunt: sit a feed edge at dawn and dusk, count points (Tip)</td></tr><tr><td>Early November</td><td>Seeking phase: bucks cruise, daylight sightings rise <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></td><td>Sit funnels and saddles all morning. Rattle and grunt for white tails (Tip)</td></tr><tr><td>Mid November</td><td>Peak deer rut, mule deer peak about now <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span></td><td>Find the does, the bucks are with them. Be patient, do not shoot the first buck (Tip)</td></tr><tr><td>Late November to 10 December</td><td>Tending and post rut. Bucks worn, snow deep up high <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span></td><td>Hunt winter range slopes, track in snow, respect the deer (Tip)</td></tr><tr><td>Mid December</td><td>Region 3 mule deer closed after 10 December; white tailed deer bow only 11 to 20 December (99%, Hunting Regulation, Schedule 3). Second rut for unbred does <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span></td><td>Scout and learn. Shed hunting comes later (Tip)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Check the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Synopsis\">Synopsis</button> for the exact season end in your MU. Region 3 mule deer buck seasons run to 10 December with a <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"4 point buck\">4 point buck</button> rule from 1 November (99%, Hunting Regulation, Schedule 3, items 5 and 6, BC Laws, checked 2026-10-17): any buck 1 to 31 October, 4 points or better 10 to 30 September and 1 November to 10 December.</li></ul></details>","<aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No electronic calls, except listed predators and Schedule C birds <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span></td><td>page 12, item 41</td></tr><tr><td>No cervid parts or urine from outside BC for hunting <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span></td><td>page 12, item 51</td></tr><tr><td>No hunting from 1 hour after sunset to 1 hour before sunrise <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></td><td>page 11, item 38</td></tr><tr><td>Do not alter antlers in antler restricted seasons <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span></td><td>page 12, item 54</td></tr><tr><td>No lights for hunting <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span></td><td>page 11, item 38</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the rules in full</summary><ul><li>Electronic or recorded calls: unlawful except for wolf, coyote, cougar, lynx, bobcat and Schedule C birds <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Synopsis page 12, item 41.</li><li>Cervid parts and derivatives from outside BC: unlawful to use for hunting (item 51), and possession is limited (item 50) <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Synopsis page 12.</li><li>Hunting hours for game other than migratory birds: unlawful from one hour after sunset to one hour before sunrise <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>. Synopsis page 11, item 38 (2 October 2026 edition). Rutting bucks move at night; you cannot.</li><li>Antler restricted seasons (spike fork moose, 6 point elk, 4 point deer): do not alter or tamper with antlers until the carcass reaches home, a meat cutter, cold storage, or compulsory inspection <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Synopsis page 12, item 54.</li><li>Hunting with a light or illuminating device is unlawful <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>. Synopsis page 11, the second item 38 (2 October 2026 edition).</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>In the rut, hunt the does. The buck will find them before you find him.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Blowing a grunt call every 2 minutes. Call little, listen a lot.</li><li>Pouring bottled deer urine on a scrape. It is unlawful if it came from outside BC, and nearly all of it does.</li><li>Quitting at 9 am in November. Rutting bucks move all morning.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>In the second week of November, sit a saddle or bench between two doe groups from first light to 11 am. Rattle once an hour for 1 minute, then sit still for 30. Log every deer and its direction in the Journal.</p></aside></details>","<figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-md-doe-front.jpg\" alt=\"In the rut, find the does first.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>In the rut, find the does first. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mule_Deer_Doe_on_Seedskadee_National_Wildlife_Refuge_(22181735559).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>In the rut, hunt the does. The buck will find them before you find him.</p></aside><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Blowing a grunt call every 2 minutes. Call little, listen a lot.</li><li>Pouring bottled deer urine on a scrape. It is unlawful if it came from outside BC, and nearly all of it does.</li><li>Quitting at 9 am in November. Rutting bucks move all morning.</li></ul></aside>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Step 1: find the line. A trail or funnel runs between a known bed in the timber and a known feeding field.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"40\" width=\"400\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#8a9a6b\"/>\n<g fill=\"#2f3a2b\"><polygon points=\"30,100 42,62 54,100\"/><polygon points=\"90,95 102,57 114,95\"/><polygon points=\"300,100 312,62 324,100\"/><polygon points=\"355,95 367,57 379,95\"/></g>\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"215\" width=\"400\" height=\"85\" fill=\"#e9dfa8\"/>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#6b4f35\">Feed</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\">Bed, uphill</text>\n<path d=\"M180 105 C 220 140, 150 180, 200 225\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8b5a2b\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"28\" font-size=\"19\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1. Find the line</text>\n<rect x=\"140\" y=\"95\" width=\"100\" height=\"140\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"250\" y=\"170\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#e8590c\">The line</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\">bed to feed</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Second week of November: sit a saddle between two doe groups, first light to 11 am. Rattle 1 minute an hour, then sit still 30. Log every deer.</p></aside>","<ul><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">full synopsis</a>, read directly from the downloaded synopsis text: page 11 items 38 (hours) and 38 (lights), page 12 items 41, 50, 51 and 54; page 15 CWD transport restrictions; page 81 Chronic Wasting Disease; Region 3 page 32 CWD testing and page 34 moose seasons. Checked 2026-10-01 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">95%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Search previews (direct reading blocked in this environment), checked 2026-10-01:<ul><li><a href=\"https://www.outdoorlife.com/hunting/how-to-rattle-deer-hunting/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Outdoor Life, rattling research by Hellickson</a> and <a href=\"https://deerassociation.com/10-tips-rattling-bucks/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">National Deer Association, rattling tips</a> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.huntingbc.ca/forum/archive/index.php/t-23985.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HuntingBC forum, moose rut timing</a> and <a href=\"https://arcadiaoutfitting.tawk.help/article/best-seasons-and-locations-for-moose-hunting-in-bc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Arcadia Outfitting, moose seasons in BC</a> (hunter reports, 60% to 70%).</li><li><a href=\"https://www.huntingbc.ca/forum/printthread.php?t=20207&amp;pp=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HuntingBC forum, elk rut in the East Kootenay</a> and <a href=\"https://www.onxmaps.com/hunt/blog/elk-rut\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">onX, when is the elk rut</a> (60% to 70%).</li><li><a href=\"https://wildsafebc.com/species/deer/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WildSafeBC, deer</a> and <a href=\"https://www.crittercarewildlife.org/the-black-tailed-deer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Critter Care, black tailed deer</a> <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>: black tailed deer rut.</li><li><a href=\"https://realtree.com/articles/deer-hunting/the-ultimate-guide-to-hunting-scrapes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Realtree, hunting scrapes</a> <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>: scrape timing.</li></ul></li></ul><ul><li>Rut timing: BC Ministry of Transportation and Infrastructure and Penn State, search previews <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Calling tactics are hunter lore, labelled Tip or 60%.</li></ul>"]